---
term: Attribution problem
section: open-problems
order: 50
definition: The difficulty of separating the effects of an executive's decisions from the effects of outside events.
seeAlso:
  - fidelity-audit
  - measurement-mechanism
---
An executive that members judge by results has a reason to claim credit for good luck and to blame bad results on outside shocks. Comparing a corporation with its [[sibling corporation|sibling corporations]] helps, as a [[fidelity audit]] does. Comparisons don't settle the question, because no two corporations face the same conditions.

Program evaluation uses the same name for the same problem. Paul Holland called its root the *fundamental problem of causal inference* (1986): nobody can observe what would have happened to the same unit under a different policy.
